{"reference":"Joel 2","book":"Joel","chapter":2,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Joel 2 moves from alarm to restoration and then to a future horizon. Its opening portrays the day of Jehovah through darkness, fire, an ordered advancing force, cosmic disturbance, and Jehovah’s voice. The force may continue the locust imagery, represent a human army, portray Jehovah’s army, or deliberately fuse these images; the text does not settle the question.","verse_refs":["Joel 2:1-11","Joel 2:18-27","Joel 2:28-32"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_joel_002_8ff555635bad2f562389a6ab"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"The response in verses 18–27 is expressed in material, communal terms: grain, wine, oil, rain, pasture, fruit-bearing trees, and food enough to remove shame. The land and animals are addressed alongside the people, so restoration is pictured as renewal of the whole damaged environment rather than as an abstract promise.","verse_refs":["Joel 2:1-11","Joel 2:18-27","Joel 2:28-32"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_joel_002_8ff555635bad2f562389a6ab"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"A communal call to return","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"The trumpet summons a fast and solemn assembly involving every part of the community, from elders and children to a newly married couple. Priests are to lament and intercede for the people. The appeal asks for mercy without identifying particular victims or groups as responsible for the crisis.","verse_refs":["Joel 2:12-17","Joel 2:18-27","Joel 2:28-32"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_joel_002_dde2a8580c9e98d848858e1b"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_4","text":"“Rend your heart, and not your garments” contrasts an inwardly real return with an empty public display. It does not reject bodily expressions of grief or lament. Likewise, the possibility that Jehovah may relent is offered as hope grounded in his character, not as a ritual formula that can compel a desired outcome.","verse_refs":["Joel 2:12-17","Joel 2:28-32"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_joel_002_68ec013e2661fa8031a4b2b0"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Questions the chapter leaves open","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"Verse 18 can be read as reporting a divine response, announcing one still to come, or marking a hinge in the chapter’s composition. The “northern” threat in verse 20 is also not securely identifiable as one particular army, ruler, or future figure. These uncertainties make it wise not to force the chapter into a single detailed sequence of events.","verse_refs":["Joel 2:18-27","Joel 2:28-32"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_joel_002_a97ff1992bc727ea3c3f19f6"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_6","text":"The four locust names in verse 25 emphasize comprehensive consumption, but they cannot securely be assigned to four species or four stages of a locust’s life. The chapter also does not name a catalog of offenses behind the disaster, so it should not be used to identify sufferers of catastrophe as uniquely guilty.","verse_refs":["Joel 2:1-27","Joel 2:12-17","Joel 2:18-27"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_joel_002_e112cac7da21813f09cd7525"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"things_easy_to_miss","title":"The Spirit oracle","blocks":[{"id":"block_7","text":"“All flesh” is immediately illustrated through sons and daughters, old and young, and male and female slaves. The full geographic scope of the phrase is interpreted differently, but the specified groups make the promise socially expansive rather than limited to a single age, gender, or status group.","verse_refs":["Joel 2:18-27","Joel 2:28-32"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_joel_002_a97ff1992bc727ea3c3f19f6"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_8","text":"Chapter numbering can differ across editions: the Spirit oracle is Joel 3:1–5 in the common four-chapter Masoretic division, but Joel 2:28–32 in many English Bibles and in the Old Greek’s three-chapter division. Peter’s Pentecost speech quotes the Greek Joel text with interpretive adaptations, including the phrase “in the last days.”","verse_refs":["Joel 2:28-32"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_joel_002_e3a24e1522c8a3e9b3773aa5"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}